Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:03.540
And then it's possible to start to
feel frustrated because I continue

00:00:03.540 --> 00:00:05.190
not understanding this stuff.

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:10.260
Hi, Steve Kaufmann here, and today I want
to talk about focus in language learning.

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:10.920
All right?

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:16.890
And the reason I'm doing that is because,
recently I've had, sort of, problems...

00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:19.380
oh, by the way, if you enjoy
my videos, please click on

00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:21.450
the bell for notifications.

00:00:21.450 --> 00:00:24.900
You know, please subscribe and
if you follow me on a podcast

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:26.550
service, please leave a comment.

00:00:26.550 --> 00:00:27.240
I do appreciate it.

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:28.509
So focus.

00:00:28.890 --> 00:00:31.230
So I was having trouble with my camera.

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:33.750
All of a sudden it was all out of focus.

00:00:34.065 --> 00:00:39.985
So the background was outta focus and
even where I was speaking was kind of out

00:00:39.985 --> 00:00:42.495
of focus and I couldn't get it to work.

00:00:42.495 --> 00:00:46.185
And no matter what I did, I
couldn't get the focus sharp

00:00:46.190 --> 00:00:47.205
the way it's supposed to be.

00:00:47.715 --> 00:00:51.105
Now, fortunately, my son was over
and he grabbed the camera and he

00:00:51.110 --> 00:00:55.575
identified by deduction that, you know,
all of the stuff that I was trying

00:00:55.575 --> 00:00:58.645
to change, you know, auto focus this
and that, I'd never touched before.

00:00:58.645 --> 00:01:04.170
So, maybe Dad, it's not so smart to think
that that's gonna solve the problem.

00:01:04.530 --> 00:01:05.489
It's something else.

00:01:05.489 --> 00:01:09.030
And sure enough, there was a
setting that, you know, that you

00:01:09.030 --> 00:01:13.649
kind of, either you were in VA
for, I guess video or something.

00:01:13.890 --> 00:01:14.670
That's where I was.

00:01:14.670 --> 00:01:15.750
That's not where I was supposed to be.

00:01:15.810 --> 00:01:17.610
I was supposed to be
on something else, A+.

00:01:18.040 --> 00:01:21.870
So with that, and I haven't checked
it, but I'm hoping that this is clearly

00:01:21.870 --> 00:01:27.150
in focus, but I thought about the
subject of focus in language learning.

00:01:27.150 --> 00:01:33.150
When we learn a language, we kind of, and
I find that I sort of move from being very

00:01:33.150 --> 00:01:39.900
focused on specific details to allowing,
you know, the language just to come in me,

00:01:39.990 --> 00:01:42.600
into my brain without tremendous focus.

00:01:42.780 --> 00:01:49.950
So, for example, I listen to my,
uh, you know, Lebanese TV program.

00:01:50.370 --> 00:01:52.200
I don't really understand that much of it.

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:53.700
I kind of have understand it.

00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:57.750
I'm starting, things are starting to
come into focus to the extent that I

00:01:57.840 --> 00:02:02.940
can hear clearly where one word, uh,
ends and the next word begins, but

00:02:02.940 --> 00:02:04.710
I don't necessarily know that word.

00:02:04.830 --> 00:02:08.580
But it is sort of an increasing
focus that's developing, but it's

00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:13.000
developing as I am allowing this
stuff to come in somewhat unfocused,

00:02:13.425 --> 00:02:18.135
and then I will go in and study
that lesson sentence by sentence.

00:02:18.135 --> 00:02:22.095
When you're studying sentence by sentence,
of course you're very much focused.

00:02:22.725 --> 00:02:29.175
And the more focused I am, the more
I do the sort of, um, vocabulary

00:02:29.955 --> 00:02:35.175
exercises, like the matching pairs or
trying to reconstitute the sentence,

00:02:35.175 --> 00:02:38.025
which the, the sentence gets scrambled
I have to put it back together.

00:02:38.760 --> 00:02:42.420
All of this is allowing me to focus in on
the language, focusing in on the language

00:02:42.420 --> 00:02:47.490
of course, I am not acquiring as many new
words because when I'm going through the

00:02:47.495 --> 00:02:51.060
whole text, it has a lot of blue words.

00:02:51.330 --> 00:02:54.630
I'm running into these yellow words
that I've saved before I'm running

00:02:54.635 --> 00:02:59.110
into them again, not necessarily
remembering, remembering them, but by

00:02:59.115 --> 00:03:04.740
running into them again, I am sort of
somewhat strengthening my hold on them.

00:03:05.160 --> 00:03:10.515
So, you know, I'm sort of pushed back
and forth or pulled back and forth.

00:03:10.520 --> 00:03:16.365
So when I'm in the sort of unfocused
study mode, a lot of stuff is coming in.

00:03:16.365 --> 00:03:20.385
It's a little bit fuzzy, uh,
gradually getting a little less fuzzy.

00:03:20.390 --> 00:03:25.605
It has new words, it has a lot of yellow
words that I have still not learned,

00:03:26.355 --> 00:03:30.105
but I'm covering a lot of material and
then I go and listen to it while I'm

00:03:30.105 --> 00:03:34.515
doing the dishes, and I'm also covering
that material in a unfocused way.

00:03:35.250 --> 00:03:38.640
And then it's possible to start to
feel frustrated because I continue

00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:40.320
not understanding this stuff.

00:03:41.010 --> 00:03:45.899
However, if I then focus in on a
particular issue, like a particular

00:03:45.899 --> 00:03:49.709
sentence or maybe a particular
grammatical issue or, or, or any

00:03:49.709 --> 00:03:55.049
particular sort of smaller sub, you
know, smaller detail in the language,

00:03:55.529 --> 00:03:58.760
I'm always conscious of the fact, yeah
but I'm not acquiring new vocabulary.

00:03:59.140 --> 00:04:03.600
So I'm pulled one way and then the
other, either I, I wanna get exposed

00:04:03.600 --> 00:04:06.570
to the language, I wanna learn
new words, I want to increase...

00:04:06.570 --> 00:04:09.900
I referred the other day to
sort of my reserve that our

00:04:09.905 --> 00:04:11.790
memory can consist of a reserve.

00:04:11.790 --> 00:04:15.750
Things that have come in, that they're in
reserve, but we can't yet retrieve them.

00:04:16.350 --> 00:04:20.580
Uh, but if we focus on just making
sure we can retrieve those, then

00:04:20.580 --> 00:04:24.420
we're gonna end up with a much
narrower, focused sort of subset.

00:04:24.734 --> 00:04:28.965
So I think I often talk about the
need to vary what we do in language

00:04:28.965 --> 00:04:35.474
learning, and I think to vary between
sort of focusing on a small part of the

00:04:35.474 --> 00:04:41.174
language, trying to, you know, focus in
on a pattern, focus in on vocabulary,

00:04:41.179 --> 00:04:43.455
focus in on a particular sentence.

00:04:43.905 --> 00:04:48.104
You still have to mix in with
that, the sort of background.

00:04:48.104 --> 00:04:48.465
So...

00:04:48.914 --> 00:04:50.474
and the background is part of it.

00:04:50.479 --> 00:04:54.974
You, even if you're very clear, like if
I have a clear picture right now on the

00:04:54.974 --> 00:04:59.205
camera and I haven't had a look at it,
it's okay if the books in the background

00:04:59.685 --> 00:05:05.414
are a little fuzzy and, and so I'm focused
in the, in the lens, but you also need the

00:05:05.414 --> 00:05:11.655
background and, and we also need that sort
of building up of that larger, you know,

00:05:11.655 --> 00:05:17.429
getting used to the language even though
it's still not really very much in focus.

00:05:17.909 --> 00:05:21.750
So, uh, I just mentioned this because
I was thinking about focusing my camera

00:05:21.750 --> 00:05:25.679
and then I thought about, you know, it,
it does help me even though it slows me

00:05:25.679 --> 00:05:29.729
down to go sentence by sentence and pick
through it and work on the words and

00:05:29.940 --> 00:05:35.379
reassemble the sentence, which kind of
shows me some aspects of grammar because

00:05:35.400 --> 00:05:40.979
I have to try to remember or even think
through which word follows which word.

00:05:41.669 --> 00:05:48.540
And while it's slowing me down I think
it is allowing me to focus in better

00:05:48.545 --> 00:05:54.390
when I listen to the, the sort of
undifferentiated or the, the, the sort

00:05:54.390 --> 00:05:58.050
of larger picture where there are many
new words or many words that I don't

00:05:58.050 --> 00:06:03.480
know, but I, I get the feeling that
I'm better able to focus in on these

00:06:03.510 --> 00:06:08.580
even because of the fact that I did
focus in on sentences for a while.

00:06:08.640 --> 00:06:13.890
So I tend to go back and forth on
being very focused at times and

00:06:13.890 --> 00:06:18.900
then allowing the language to come
at me in a sort of unfocused way.

00:06:19.470 --> 00:06:24.540
So I don't know whether that's helpful,
but I think it's important that whatever

00:06:24.540 --> 00:06:28.200
we're doing in the language, we want
to feel, at least, I want to feel

00:06:28.200 --> 00:06:30.370
that what I'm doing is worthwhile.

00:06:30.630 --> 00:06:32.760
It's a worthwhile use of my time.

00:06:33.240 --> 00:06:39.180
And so for that reason, I have to remind
myself that, yeah, I keep on listening

00:06:39.180 --> 00:06:41.400
to the, you know, the whole story.

00:06:42.060 --> 00:06:46.740
Uh, the whole television interview,
and I keep on not understanding

00:06:46.740 --> 00:06:51.930
it, but it doesn't matter because
that's building up my ability to see

00:06:51.930 --> 00:06:53.700
it more clearly, to focus better.

00:06:54.030 --> 00:07:00.030
And on the other hand, when I focus in
on a more limited bit of the language,

00:07:00.060 --> 00:07:04.110
like a sentence or some aspect of
the language, it's slowing me down.

00:07:04.110 --> 00:07:06.080
I'm not creating as many new LingQs.

00:07:06.360 --> 00:07:07.770
I'm not reading as much.

00:07:08.610 --> 00:07:14.190
Uh, but by focusing in on these sort
of, these samples of the language,

00:07:14.370 --> 00:07:21.870
it is helping me focus again on the
broader, uh, sort of range of, of,

00:07:21.900 --> 00:07:23.580
of language input that I'm getting.

00:07:23.640 --> 00:07:26.370
So I don't know if that's helpful to you.

00:07:26.430 --> 00:07:29.790
Uh, it's something that I
felt was meaningful to me.

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:34.230
It's a little bit, I should perhaps sit
down and think it through and, and do

00:07:34.230 --> 00:07:36.900
some more research on it, but I just
thought I would share that with you.

00:07:37.110 --> 00:07:38.760
So thank you for listening.

00:07:39.250 --> 00:07:39.765
Bye for now.

